A LIST ITEM HOLDING ONLY A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION LOSES BOTH.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The whole list goes, and the URL with it:

    "# Title\n\n"

`md blocks` says `unordered list(0 items)` on the input, so the item is gone
before the writer ever sees it.

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Write the item and its definition back. The URL appears
nowhere else in the file, so once `format` writes the file it is gone for
good.

THE NEIGHBOUR THAT ALREADY OWNS HALF OF THIS.
`format-keeps-an-unused-link-reference-definition` tracks the same loss at the
top level of a document, and states the cause: cmark-gfm consumes a link
reference definition while parsing and stores it in the document's reference
map rather than leaving a node in the tree, so `MarkdownParser.parse` never
sees one. Keeping it needs the definitions read out of the SOURCE before the
tree is walked, because the parser will not hand them back.

WHY THIS CASE IS NOT A DUPLICATE. Inside a list item the loss takes a second
thing with it. The item has no children left once cmark has eaten the
definition, so the item produces nothing, the LIST empties, and a block that
the author wrote disappears whole. At the top level only the definition is
lost and the surrounding document survives.

READ THIS BEFORE FIXING `format-keeps-an-empty-list-item`. THE TWO INTERACT.
That defect is an item with no children being dropped, and the obvious fix is
to emit an item when the item node has no children, since that is how an item
the author wrote empty looks. An item whose only content was a link reference
definition looks EXACTLY the same to that test, because cmark removed the node.
So the obvious fix turns this input into:

    # Title

    -

which is worse than today: the URL is still gone AND a bare bullet is invented
in its place, which is the same class of defect as the one this branch just
fixed. Whichever lands second has to tell the two apart, and the source text
is the only place that can: the line under the item says `[ref]: /url`, and an
item the author wrote empty has nothing after its marker.
